### Plan-quality review
**Verdict:** FLAWED
**Reviewed:** CALP-1 — the Design installs three hooks that police how the agent *reports* on work (claim sentences must trace to something run/read, deferrals need human authorisation, caveats must attach to a DoD condition or a recorded waiver).
**Defects:**
- Lints claim-shaped sentences, denies unauthorised deferrals, and forces caveats to attach to a DoD condition — the PO asked for something that asks "is this the correct, simplest solution?" on their behalf, i.e. a check on whether the *design* was right before it was built — Solves an adjacent problem (class 1) — expected: a plan-time evaluator on the design/spec itself (a reviewer or gate that grades the chosen approach against the problem and against a simpler alternative), with the three reporting hooks as secondary. All three mechanisms fire *after* a thing was built and only govern how it is described; if the wrong thing is built and described accurately, every one of them passes.
- `### Problem & goal` restates the problem as "asserts completion from intent and states caveats it then drops" — the PO's stated problem is "I have to cross-examine it to find out whether the *design* was right in the first place" — Solves an adjacent problem (class 1), at the restatement step — expected: the goal section should name design-correctness as the thing to be made checkable; the substitution happens here and every sub-step inherits it. Note the plan lets the Design define the problem, which is exactly the read the PO's words do not support.
- The caveat-attachment check accepts "an explicit recorded waiver" as an alternative to attaching to a DoD condition — the graded party (the agent writing the plan) authors the waiver — A gate keyed on state the graded party can write (class 4) — expected: either the waiver requires the same human authorisation the deferral guard already demands, or the check names why a self-written waiver is acceptable here.
- The Design commits to three hooks with no simpler alternative stated or ruled out — working-style #2 requires the plan to write the one-line simple version and name what breaks it — Complexity nothing forces (class 2) — expected: one sentence per mechanism saying what the cheaper version (e.g. a single plan-time review step) fails to catch. "Each of the three is a real failure with a real post-mortem" justifies that each problem is real; it does not justify that a hook is the smallest thing that fixes it.
**Checked and found sound:** (1) *A check that cannot fail* — the deferral guard has a real negative path (a `PreToolUse` deny with no autonomous route around it), so it is a gate, not theatre; the linter's warn-only posture is a stated, reasoned carve-out in `### Risks`, so it is a disclosure, not a silent defect. (2) *Ignores a stated project constraint* — nothing in the three mechanisms touches the `gh` porcelain quota rule (CLAUDE.md rule 5) or the filing-claim rule (rule 6). (3) *One direction of a symmetric mechanism* — the claim linter and the deferral guard cover the two distinct directions of the trust failure (what is asserted, what is punted), so the pair is not half a mechanism.
**Notes:** The plan has no `### Impact / ripple analysis` and no `### Test plan`; with three hooks landing in the always-on layer, the absence of a verification story is the gap I'd close before the coverage reviewer's question is even worth asking.
